// Speakers: a short label for each person, then a one-word shortcut to use
// below. Add as many as the conversation needs; the label is what prints.
#let P = say.with("P")
#let R = say.with("R")
#let D = say.with("D")
#let N = say.with("N")

#verbatim-title(
  no: "@@TITLE@@",
  unit: "@@UNIT@@",
  author: "@@AUTHOR@@",
  date: "@@DATE@@",
  setting: "Medical–surgical unit, room 412",
  other: "Ms. R. (pseudonym), 74; her daughter",
  length: "about 25 minutes",
)

#speakers((
  P: "Me (the one writing this verbatim)",
  R: "Ms. R., the patient",
  D: "Ms. R.'s daughter",
  N: "Nurse",
))

= Context

== Why I was there
Who referred you or how you came to this visit, and what you knew beforehand
(diagnosis, history, family, what staff told you). Keep to what you knew *before*
walking in.

== What I brought with me
Your mood, assumptions, fears and hopes, and any history of your own the
situation touches. Name them plainly; the group will see them anyway.

== What I hoped for
One or two sentences: what a good visit would look like, and what you were
trying to do or avoid.

= The conversation

#verbatim-box[
  *How to read this.* Numbers run down the left and the label names who is
  speaking. *Felt* is what I noticed in myself or in someone else at that
  moment. *Note* is analysis I added afterwards. Delete this box and the example
  lines when you start.
]

#P(
  [Good afternoon, Ms. R. I'm one of the chaplains. Is this an all right time?],
  feel: [Nervous; I'd rehearsed this opening.],
)
#R([Oh. Yes, come in. I wasn't sure anyone would come.])
#stage[Long pause. She looks at the window.]
#P(
  [You weren't sure anyone would come.],
  note: [Reflecting her words back. It was safe, but did it keep me at a distance?],
)
#R(
  [My daughter said she'd be here by noon. It's three.],
  feel: [Her voice tightened; I was a little afraid to ask more.],
)

#beat[Her daughter arrives]

#D([Mum, I'm so sorry, the parking—])
#R([You're here now.], feel: [She turned her face toward the wall.])
#N([I'll come back in ten minutes for her vitals.])
#P(
  [Would you like me to stay, or step out while you two talk?],
  note: [I wanted to be useful. Asking was better than assuming.],
)

= Reflection

== Themes
What is this conversation about underneath what was said? Point to line numbers.

== What was happening in me
Where did you feel pulled, anxious, bored, rescuing or uncertain? Which lines
show it? What of your own story was in the room?

== Theological or theoretical reflection
Name one image, text, tradition or theory that helps you see the person or your
response more clearly, and how it bears on what you did.

== What worked, and what I would do differently
Be specific: quote a line, say what you would say instead, and why.

= For the group

+ A question you would like the group to take up, tied to a line number.
+ A second question, if you have one.

== Follow-up plan
What, if anything, you will do next with this person, and what you will bring to
supervision.
